The attached image illustrates the difference in local inflammation at injection sites in mice two weeks post-injection, comparing aluminum hydroxide (AH) adjuvant with Nano-11 adjuvant. The left panel, representing AH, shows extensive granulomatous inflammation (*) within the skeletal muscle (M). In contrast, the right panel, representing Nano-11, reveals significantly less residual inflammation, with only relatively few macrophages arranged in slender cords between muscle fibers (*). This histological comparison supports the PDF's finding that Nano-11 induces a mild and transient inflammatory response that resolves more quickly than the more persistent inflammation caused by aluminum hydroxide adjuvant, indicating a better safety profile for Nano-11 as a vaccine adjuvant.